## Introduction
Hydrogen is a promising energy carrier coming with high energy density and clean-burning features and hydrogen production by water electrolysis has gained surging interest.[1] Generally, water electrolysis is operated at acidic media for proton exchange membrane fuel cells, at basic media for the commercially used alkaline electrolysis and at neutral media for the sea-water electrolysis and microbial electrolysis cells. Particularly, as hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) proceeds, the proton concentration variation is inevitable. Thus, an idea HER catalyst is anticipated to function effectively at multi-pH conditions.[2] So far, Pt-based materials are the best HER catalysts due to the near-zero Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption $(\Delta G_{\mathsf{H}^{\star}\to0})$ , allowing easy hydrogen absorption and desorption. However, their high cost and poor stability hinder the large-scale deployment.[3] Thus, considerable effort has been made to explore efficient and durable non-Pt alternatives. The existed efficient non-Pt HER catalysts are mainly transition metal-based materials, most of which present very high HER activity only in acidic media. Moreover, inherent metal leaching or corrosion susceptibility in strong acidic/basic environments overtime often impedes the long-term durability.[4] Hence, it is urgent to develop efficient and robust metal-free all-pH HER catalysts for sustainable HER with broad application scopes.
Metal-free nanocarbons are promising catalysts for various energy-related reactions due to low cost, tuneable electronic structures and good tolerance to acidic/basic media.[5] However, most nanocarbons, even after doping with single heteroatom (e.g. B, N) are generally inactive for HER due to inferior $\Delta G_{H^{\star}}$ and relatively hydrophobic nature. Encouragingly, some advances were achieved by regulating the surface and electronic structure, such as doping N-rich carbon with a second heteroatom (i.e. B, P, S) or coupling it with catalytically active $C_{3}N_{4}$ , which enhanced the HER activity but limited to only acidic electrolyte.[6] To date, whether metal-free nanocarbons could produce similar good catalytic behaviour in both basic and neutral media as that in acid remains unknown.[7] Especially, allpH catalytic potential of nanocarbons is rarely explored and the nature of HER active sites remains elusive. Thus, a rational strategy for nanocarbon surface/electronic modulation is urgently required to implement efficient metal-free pH-universal HER catalysis while providing a lucid mechanistic insight.
Herein, taking N-doped carbon (MHCF) as our paradigm, we adopt the concept of combined intra-/inter-molecular electron transfer to engineer its electronic structure and turn inactive MHCF into efficient and robust all-pH HER catalysts by paring MHCF (acceptors) and higher-Fermi-level pure CNTs (donors) into electronically-coupled donor-acceptor ensembles, the surface of which can be readily modulated by carboxyl-grouprich polymer (e. g. poly (ethylene-alt-maleic acid), PEMAc) modification to further boost all-pH activities. The Fermi level difference of two carbon allotropes triggers interfacial charge separation, which, combined with N-induced intramolecular charge transfer in MHCF, synergistically tunes hydrogen adsorption and desorption, while the PEMAc modification not only increases the local proton concentration and buffer capacity in acidic media, but also promotes adsorption and activation of water in basic and neutral media. As a result of combined surface and dual electronic modulation, as-obtained ternary hybrid membrane exhibits remarkably enhanced HER activities along with unprecedented durability ( $\leq5\%$ activity loss over 100 h) simultaneously at all pH conditions in stark contrast to three inactive components. Density function theory (DFT) calculation unveils that synergistic electronic modulation induced by CNTs and N dopants regulates $\Delta G_{H^{\star}}$ close to zero and renders C atom adjacent to pyrrolic N in MHCF as the most active site.
## Results and Discussion
