Figure 1a illustrates the preparation process of MHCFCNTs@PEMAc and MHCF-CNTs hybrid membranes. Typically, MHCF was first produced using zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) as the precursor by template-directed self-assembly and subsequent annealing treatment in Ar atmosphere and template removal using our recently-developed strategy.[8a] CNTs with the diameters of $\sim15\ \mathsf{n m}$ were subjected to purification to remove metal impurities by hydrochloric acid (Figure S1-2). Next, a uniform dispersion was prepared by sonicating a certain amount of MHCF/CNTs (mass ratio of 1:1) mixture in an aqueous solution followed by the addition of PEMAc. Afterwards, the obtained mixture dispersion was cast onto a clean vessel. Finally, a self-standing nanoporous hybrid membrane (MHCFCNTs@PEMAc) was constructed after the solvent evaporation and mechanically peeling, serving as free-standing electrodes (Figure S3). Our preparation process is simple and readily scaled up (Figure 1b). For comparison, individual MCHF and CNTs membrane as well as the CNTs@PEMAc and MHCFCNTs hybrid membrane were also produced by similar protocol. Different MHCFs produced at different annealing temperature (800, 950 and $1100^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}})$ were used to produce the hybrid membranes, while the hybrid membrane with different MHCF/CNT mass ratios (1:2, 2:3, 1:1, 3:2, 2:1) were also prepared. Here, it is noted that the samples with the carbonization temperature of $950^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for MHCF and the MHCF/CNTs mass ratio of 1:1 were mainly used for the following demonstration unless otherwise stated.
The scanning electron microscopic (SEM) and transmission electron microscopic (TEM) imaging of individual MHCF reveals an ordered macroporous carbon framework with the macropore size of $200\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Figure S4b and Figure 1c). The presence of such macropores was confirmed by using mercury porosimetry. (Figure S5). After the incorporation of CNTs, the threedimensional (3D) backbone of MHCF is preserved, while highly porous and interpenetrated network architecture is created (Figure 1d), wherein 1D conductive nanotubes (diameter: $\mathord{\sim}15$ nm) are partly embedded inside macropore channels and serve as a connecting bridge across the backbone, thereby facilitating rapid electron transfer within MHCF-CNTs. Similar morphology was also observed in MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc (Figure 1e).
Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy mapping indicates a uniform distribution of N, C and O elements. N comes from the ZIF-8 precursor while O mainly arises from the oxygen-carrying groups of PEMAc (Figure 1f), consistent with the weight loss in thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc (Figure S6). High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) observation reveals an ultrathin polymer coating layer (2 nm in thickness) on the nanotube surface via van der Waals force and hydrophobic interaction (Figure S7). Furthermore, the MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc membrane shows a much smaller contact angle $(93^{\circ})$ relative to MHCF-CNTs (152) arising from the hydrophilic nature of carboxyl groups from the polymer side chains analyzed from Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Figure S8-9). Nitrogen adsorption-desorption curves uncover that MHCF-CNTs has a large specific surface area (SSA) of $698\ m^{2}\ {\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ with mesomicroporosity, which is higher than those of some reported carbon/CNT composites produced by different methods.[8b,8c] This facilitates the mass transfer and accessibility of active sites. In spite of the polymer coating, MHCF-CNTs $@$ PEMAc still retains meso-microporosity with a SSA of 181 $\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S10 and Table S1).
The electrocatalytic HER performance of the as-obtained catalysts on glassy carbon electrodes (GCE) was examined by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves in 1 M KOH, $0.5~\mathsf{M}$ ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ and 1 M phosphate buffered saline (PBS, $p H=7$ respectively (Figure 2a-c). All the potentials were calibrated with respect to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) and all currents were corrected by $95\%$ iR compensation. We investigated the effects of carbonization temperature and the MHCF/CNT mass ratio on the catalytic performance and it was found that the optimal annealing condition for MHCF is $950~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and the optimal mass ratio is 1:1. (Figure S11-12, see more details in supporting information). Clearly, MHCF and CNTs have negligible HER activities in each electrolyte. Surprisingly, direct coupling of MHCF and CNTs yields a metal-free pHuniversal catalyst for HER, which can function in 1.0 M KOH, $0.5\mathsf{M}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and 1 M PBS, requiring the overpotentials of 351,
392 and $428~\mathsf{m V}$ at $10\ m A\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . Notably, incorporating the hydrophilic PEMAc into MHCF-CNTs further boosts the HER activities of MHCF-CNTs and lowers the overpotentials at 10 mA $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ in all three electrolytes to 289, 317 and $278\mathrm{\mV},$ , respectively in contrast to inactive CNTs@PEMAc and PEMAc (Figure S13-14). Given the “metal-free” nature of our catalysts, these results are comparable and even superior to those of some recently-reported metal-free HER catalyst under similar conditions (Table S2). Similar HER activities were also observed under other pH conditions $\cdot P H=1$ , 3, 5, 10, 12, Figure S15). In addition, MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc demonstrates smaller Tafel slopes of 106, 121, and $108\mathrm{~mV}\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ in KOH, ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ and PBS relative to all other counterparts including CNTs (150, 279, and 679 mV dec-1), MHCF (145, 238, and $183~\mathsf{m V}~\mathsf{d e c}^{-1};$ , and MHCF-CNTs (138, 167 and 159 mV dec-1) under identical conditions, manifesting more favorable HER kinetics of MHCFCNTs@PEMAc (Figure S16-21). Particularly in alkaline and neutral media, the reduced Tafel slopes for MHCFCNTs $@$ PEMAc imply that the Volmer reaction as the ratedetermining step proceed much faster over MHCFCNTs@PEMAc relative to other reference catalysts (Table S2). Similar to many previous reports,[9a-e] in order to achieve highly catalytic electrodes, the catalysts were loaded onto the metal foams such as nickel foam (NF) or copper foam (CuF) owing to their 3D porous structures, large surface area and superb electron transfer ability, which are often utilized as conductive substrates for practical water electrolysis devices.[9a-e] Herein, bare NF has poor activities for HER in 1 M KOH and 1 M PBS and CuF is also inactive in 0.5 M ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ (Figure S22). In sharp contrast, to afford a current density of $10\ m A\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , MHCFCNTs $@$ PEMAc loaded on NF yields ultralow overpotentials of $132~\mathsf{m V}$ in $1M\mathsf{K O H}$ and $120~\mathsf{m V}$ in 1 M PBS. Meanwhile, MHCF-CNTs $@$ PEMAc loaded on CuF also delivers a small overpotential of $180~\mathsf{m V}$ at $10\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ in $0.5~\mathsf{M}$ ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ (Figure 2a-c). Clearly, the overpotentials of samples on metal foams are markedly lower than those of samples on GCE, implying the promotional role of metal substrates, similar to previous reports.[9b, 9f] These values on metal foams are comparable and even superior to those of many literature-reported non-noblemetal HER electrocatalysts under similar conditions (Table S2), although they are still higher than that of the benchmark Pt/C catalysts. Of note, the demonstrated simultaneous high HER activities and durability at all pH values have seldom appeared in previous metal-free HER catalysts.
To examine the catalyst durability in three different electrolytes, we performed chronoamperometric tests of MHCFCNTs@PEMAc over $100\mathrm{h}$ (Figure 2d). Remarkably, the results show that the catalytic current densities retain stable at $\mathord{\sim}10\mathrm{~m~A~}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ with very little activity loss of $\leq5\%$ in each electrolyte over 100 h, manifesting superb catalytic stability of MHCFCNTs@PEMAc at all pH values. Similar result was also detected in the MHCF-CNTs catalyst $(<5\%$ activity loss after $100~\mathsf{h}.$ , Figure S23-24). Moreover, the polarization curves of MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc combined with SEM imaging, Raman, XPS and FT-IR results collected before and after continuous HER for $100\mathrm{~h~}$ exhibit negligible difference (Figure S25-28). Taken together, the above results uncover that MHCFCNTs@PEMAc can function as an efficient and robust HER catalyst at all pH conditions, comparable to many recently reported non-noble-metal HER catalysts under various pH conditions (Table S2). Notably, ultralong operation time over $100\ \mathrm{~h~}$ for MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc simultaneously realized at acidic, basic and neutral media has rarely been achieved for the ever-reported HER catalysts before. Such long-anticipated pHuniversal catalytic nature and unprecedented durability of a metal-free catalyst hold great promise to sustainable catalysis, not only saving the running cost but also rendering the catalyst broad applicability in complicated environment.
To understand the origin of remarkably enhanced HER activities in MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc, the electrochemically active surface areas (ECSAs) of the samples were estimated by measuring double-layer capacitance. MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc gives a lower ECSA relative to MHCF-CNTs (Figure S29-31), which excludes the role of ECSA towards enhanced HER activity after PEMAc modification (Figure S32). Of note, although the ppm-level residual metal (Ni, Fe, Zn) was detected in our composite by the inductively coupled plasma spectra (ICP-MS, Table S3), such trace amount of metal makes negligible contribution to the HER activities of our composite, as supported by our experimental studies and some previous reports (see Figure S33-36 and more details in supporting information). In this case, our catalysts are considered as “metal-free” ones similar to previous reports.[10a-c] XPS spectroscopy was performed to probe the possible interaction between components (Figure S37). As exhibited in Figure 3a, the high-resolution N1s spectrum for MHCF can be fitted into four subpeaks corresponding to pyridinic N $(398.8\ \mathsf{e V})$ , pyrrolic N $(400.3~\mathsf{e V})$ , quaternary N $(401.5~\mathsf{e V})$ and oxidized N (403.8 eV). By contrast, the pyridinic, pyrrolic, quaternary N peaks for MHCF-CNTs shift to lower binding energy by 0.2, 0.3 and 0.3 eV respectively, signifying the existence of the electron transfer from CNTs to MHCF, resulting in the electron enrichment over
MHCF.[10d] Raman results in Figure 3b reveal that the $\mathsf{G}$ peak at $1568~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ presents an upshift by $13\ c m^{-1}$ after coupling CNTs with MHCF, once again verifying that MHCF extracts the electrons from nanotubes after their combination.[11]
In fact, the work function $(\varphi)$ of MHCF was determined to be $\mathord{\sim}5.50\ \mathrm{\texteV}$ higher than that of CNTs $(\sim5.05~\mathsf{e V})$ by kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) wherein the contrast in the contact potential difference (CPD) image is equivalent to the local work function variation of the sample and the Au (Figure 3c-d). Thus, it is energetically favorable for the electron migration from CNTs to higher-work-function MHCF after their contact. In addition, the differential charge density of the MHCF-CNT model was also calculated by DFT simulation (Figure 3d, Figure S38-40, the blue and yellow regions in Figure 3b denote the gain and lose of the electron density). Clearly, the surface of MHCF attracts the electrons from the neighboring CNTs, coinciding with the above XPS, Raman and KPFM results. Thus, it is suggested that MHCF acts as the electron acceptor while CNTs function as the electron donor similar to the literature.[9b, 12a] Figure S41 shows the total density of states (DOS) of MHCF, CNTs and MHCF-CNTs. Among three models, MHCF-CNTs deliver the highest density of states across the Fermi level. This implies enhanced electron mobility in the composite system, which is vital for HER.[3a] It is noted that MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc exhibits almost the same spectroscopic results (XPS and Raman) as that of MHCF-CNTs (Figure 3a-b and Figure S42- S43), indicating the absence of electronic interaction of insulated PEMAc and nanocarbons. This also implies that electron transfer from CNTs to MHCF still exists even after incorporating the PEMAc, most probably enabled by electrontunneling effect.[12b]
To render further fundamental insights into the enhanced HER activity of hybrid membranes, DFT calculations were done by building up the correlated model MHCF-CNT. For comparison, similar modelling was also conducted for nondoped graphite (G), N-doped graphite (MHCF) and carbon nanotube (CNT) (Figure S44-51). It is known that $\Delta G_{H^{\star}}$ close to zero is regarded as a good candidate for HER owing to the balanced hydrogen adsorption and desorption over the catalyst surface. $[13a-d]$ Clearly, MHCF-CNT exhibits much smaller $\lvert\Delta\mathsf{G}_{\ H^{\star}}\rvert$ values $(0.143~\mathsf{e V})$ than those of all other counterparts, close to Pt (0.09 eV),[3] which leads to mediated $\mathsf{H}^{\star}$ adsorption and desorption behavior on the MHCF-CNT surface and hence promote HER, coinciding with the above experimental results (Table S4-S7). Generally, different adsorption sites and configurations could induce the change of $\Delta G_{\mathsf{H}^{\star}}$ .[12a] The adsorption site with the smallest $|\Delta G_{\mathsf{H}^{\star}}|$ could be regarded as the most active site.[3,13e] We calculated the $\Delta G_{H^{\star}}$ values of all possible adsorption sites in several models (Table S4-7). It was found that carbon atom adjacent to pyrrolic N in the MHCF-CNT model (Figure S45) has the smallest $|\Delta G_{H^{\star}}|$ (Table S4) and thus identified as the most active site in the composite catalyst, though direct coupling of MHCF and CNT reduces the $|\Delta G_{H^{\star}}|$ values over other sites such as pyrrolic and quaternary N atoms as well as some adjacent C atoms. This claim is also supported by the partial DOS analysis (Figure S52) and previous studies.[13e,13f] The quantity of the most active site of MHCFCNTs on GCE (surface area: $0.196~\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ is estimated to be ${\sim}5.69\times10^{16}$ atoms corresponding to the active site density of ${\sim}2.90\times10^{17}$ atoms $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ (see details in supporting information).
In fact, the electron-withdrawing ability of N dopants in MHCF can make adjacent carbon atoms positively charged by intra-molecular electron transfer $({\mathsf{C}}\to{\mathsf{N}})$ , as illustrated in Figure S44-51, similar to previous studies.[14] Though N-induced local charge redistribution somewhat improves hydrogen adsorption behavior relative to non-doped carbon $(\mathsf{G},1.721\ \mathsf{e V})$ , the $\Delta G_{H^{\star}}$ $(0.249~\mathsf{e V})$ on MHCF is still larger, implying rather weak $\mathsf{H}^{\star}$ adsorption (Table S4-S7). Thus, it is not surprised that MHCF alone has poor HER activity. In our case, after coupling MHCF with CNT, the intermolecular electron transfer (CNT $\rightarrow$ MHCF) enriches the surface electron density of MHCF to engender second electronic rearrangement in MHCF such that the positive charge density over C atoms adjacent to N atoms especially pyrrolic $\mathsf{N}$ is moderated (blue area observed around C sites represents electron enrichment, Figure 4a), eventually regulating the $\Delta G_{H^{\star}}$ closer to zero. Of note, when the CNT is coupled with non-doped carbon $(G)$ , the $\mathsf{G}$ -CNT yields a rather large $|\Delta G_{H^{\star}}|$ $(1.588\mathsf{e V})$ , in sharp contrast to the coupling of CNT with N-doped carbon (MHCF) with much smaller $|\Delta G_{H^{\star}}|$ (0.143 eV). These results highlight the synergetic role of N dopants and CNTs in optimizing the HER activities of MHCF by combined intra-/inter-molecular electron transfer.
Besides the above-mentioned dual electronic regulation induced by intra-/inter-molecular charge transfer, the PEMAc modification of MHCF-CNTs is also found to be critical for boosting HER activities in three electrolytes. Indeed, the XPS spectrum of MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc reveals the presence of rich carboxyl groups caused by the PEMAc (Figure S8). Hence, MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc gives negative zeta potential values at all pH conditions (Figure 4c). In acidic media, such negatively charged character can enrich the local proton concentration at catalyst/electrolyte interfaces by electrostatic interaction to render a buffer capacity and alleviate the $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ depletion during high reaction rates (Figure 4d), facilitating the HER even at lower ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ concentration.[15a] This is reflected by one uncommon fact that MHCF-CNT $@$ PEMAc exhibits almost identical overpotential and Tafel slope when the pH varies from 0 to 0.5, distinct from MHCF-CNT with marked activity fading (Figure S53-54). Notably, compared with identical process in acidic media, water adsorption and activation is a key step to affect the HER under the neutral or basic conditions.[7a-b] As can be seen in Figure S55, MHCF-CNT@PEMAc presents much smaller water contact angle than that of MHCF-CNTs, while the PEMAc modification leads to smaller water adsorption energies relative to pure MHCF and CNT (Figure 4e). These results signify favorable water affinity of MHCF-CNT@PEMAc. Importantly, Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy with the attenuated total reflection technique (FT-IR ATR) spectrum of MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc collected after water adsorption in Figure 4f shows an obvious peak at $1618~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , corresponding to the bending vibration of $0\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ bonds from the adsorbed water (Figure S56). The observed red-shift by $4~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ for the $0\cdot-H$ peak relative to MHCF-CNTs indicates that the O-H bond over MHCF-CNTs@PEMAc becomes longer, implying the effective activation of water molecules after the introduction of PEMAc.[7a] Our theoretical calculation reveals that, after polymer coating, the energy barrier of water dissociation was obviously reduced (Figure S57), which further supports that the PEMAc coating indeed activates the water dissociation.[15b,15c]
## Conclusions
In conclusion, we have showcased a combined surface and dual electronic modulation strategy to drastically boost the HER activities of N-doped carbon (e. g. MHCF) and realize efficient and robust metal-free all-pH catalysis for the first time, by paring MHCF (electron acceptors) and higher-Fermi-level CNTs (electron donors) into electronically-coupled all-carbon complex, followed by noncovalent modification of hydrophilic PEMAc. The Fermi level difference of MHCF and CNTs enables directional inter-molecular electron transfer from CNTs to MHCF, which coupled with N-triggered intra-molecular electron transfer in MHCF, synergistically tunes charge density distribution and optimizes hydrogen adsorption and desorption. Further surface modulation with carboxyl-group-rich PEMAc not only enriches the local proton concentration at electrode/electrolyte interfaces at ${\mathsf{p H}}=0,$ but also promotes water adsorption and activation at ${\mathsf{p H}}=7$ or 14. As a result, the resulting hybrid membranes yield superior HER performance featuring low overpotentials and unprecedented durability $(\leq5\%$ activity loss over 100 h)
simultaneously at the acidic, neutral and basic media, although three constituents alone are inactive. DFT calculation uncovers the synergy of CNTs and N dopants, which regulates $\Delta G_{\mathsf{H}}^{\star}$ near zero via combined intra-/inter-molecular electron transfer and renders C adjacent to pyrrolic-N in MHCF as the most active site. Our finding could shed a new light on the design of metalfree pH-universal HER catalysts. The proposed concept can be regarded as a general idea to design various donor-acceptor complexes for many energy-related reactions.
